{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1785260962,"nonce":"QrfOs2dwumCsL1W8fDJm4N4sUp4=","title":"ADR-0062: the Export routes accept any valid workspace token, so one Step can drive another Step's Export","message":"`POST /v1/exports`, `/claim`, `/{handle}/settle`, `DELETE /{handle}` and `GET /v1/exports` all authenticate with `authenticate` — any valid, unexpired workspace token. A token minted for Step A therefore authorises operations on Step B's Export.\n\nThe Export **capability** is per-Step and unguessable, so an attacker still needs the capability to *claim* an Export — but `prepare`, `settle`, `revoke` and the `live_handles` listing are reachable with any Step's token. Same class as the documented blob-read gap in the module docs: **fence-authenticated, not fence-authorized**.\n\nWritten down rather than quietly tightened because the honest scope matters: this is a lateral-movement surface between co-tenant Steps, not a path in from outside.\n\n- [ ] Decide the narrowing: a `Scope` for depot-administrative calls, or fence-matching on the route (the token's `{run, step, attempt}` must match the Export's)\n- [ ] Fence-matching is probably right for settle/revoke and wrong for `GET /v1/exports`, which is an operator/drain-then-roll view — so that one likely needs its own scope\n- [ ] A test asserting Step A's token cannot settle or revoke Step B's Export","files":null}]}